<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Launch daemon definition for AeolusHelper.

  Copied into Aeolus.app/Contents/Library/LaunchDaemons/ and registered with
  SMAppService.daemon(plistName:). Shipping the daemon inside the app bundle means
  deleting the app removes the daemon with it — the main reason to prefer SMAppService
  over the legacy SMJobBless flow.

  Do NOT add SMJobBless keys here (SMAuthorizedClients in particular). Mixing the legacy
  and modern registration schemes produces registration failures whose error messages do
  not point at the cause. Client authorisation is done in code, by checking the
  connecting process's code-signing requirement — see docs/SECURITY-adjacent notes in
  SECURITY.md and epic E2.

  ## On-demand launch only, and why there is no RunAtLoad or KeepAlive

  MachServices is the entire launch policy: launchd starts the helper when a client
  connects to the mach service, and at no other time. That is all E2 needs.

  Sources/AeolusHelper/AeolusHelperMain.swift is still a scaffold — it writes to stderr
  and exits non-zero, deliberately, so that a do-nothing process never holds root. Under
  KeepAlive = { SuccessfulExit = false } launchd restarts a job "in the inverse
  condition", i.e. whenever it exits non-zero, and launchd.plist(5) notes that key also
  implies RunAtLoad. Together those turn the scaffold's refusal into a root process
  started at approval time and at every boot, exiting 1, and restarted on launchd's
  throttle indefinitely — while SMAppService.Status reports .enabled throughout, so the
  app would say "Helper installed and enabled" about a daemon that cannot run at all.
  A refusal to run is only a refusal if nothing restarts it.

  TODO(#72): revisit boot-launch and restart policy when the helper becomes a real daemon
  with an NSXPCListener. Decide it against the self-renewing-lease semantics E5 actually
  implements — whether a lease is meant to survive a reboot, and what a crash while one is
  held should do — rather than assuming a daemon wants to be kept alive. Every key added
  here starts a root process, so this is a safety decision and not a convenience one.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.blamechris.Aeolus.Helper</string>

	<key>BundleProgram</key>
	<string>Contents/MacOS/AeolusHelper</string>

	<key>MachServices</key>
	<dict>
		<key>com.blamechris.Aeolus.Helper</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Adaptive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
